@[expose] public section

/-!
# A criterion for the completion of a module to be adically complete

Let `R` be a ring, let `I ⊆ R` be an ideal and let `M` be an `R`-module, and write `M^∧` for the
`I`-adic completion of `M`. Set `Kₙ = Ker(M^∧ → M/IⁿM)`. Then `M^∧` is `I`-adically complete if and
only if `Kₙ = IⁿM^∧` for every `n ≥ 1`.

The containment `IⁿM^∧ ⊆ Kₙ` always holds, and the family `{Kₙ}` shares three further properties
with the `I`-adic filtration `{IⁿM^∧}`: it decreases, it has zero intersection, and `Kₙ` is
generated by `Kₙ₊₁` together with `IⁿM^∧`. The last of these is the mechanism of the proof:
`IⁿM^∧` maps onto `IⁿM/Iⁿ⁺¹M`, so an element `x` of `Kₙ`, whose `(n+1)`-st component lies in
`IⁿM/Iⁿ⁺¹M` because its `n`-th component vanishes, can be corrected by an element of `IⁿM^∧` to
lie in `Kₙ₊₁`.

In a precomplete module those four properties force a filtration to *be* the `I`-adic filtration,
and that is the whole of the forward direction, `M^∧` being precomplete as soon as it is complete.
Iterating the correction from `x ∈ Kₙ` gives remainders `c₀ = x`, `cₖ₊₁ = cₖ - zₖ` with
`cₖ ∈ Kₙ₊ₖ` and `zₖ ∈ Iⁿ⁺ᵏM^∧`, so the partial sums `x - cₖ = z₀ + ⋯ + zₖ₋₁` all lie in `IⁿM^∧`
and form an `I`-adic Cauchy sequence. Their limit `L` lies in `IⁿM^∧` because `L` and `x - cₙ` are
congruent modulo `IⁿM^∧`; and `x - L = cₖ + ((x - cₖ) - L)` lies in `Kₖ` for every `k`, whence
`x = L` by the vanishing of the intersection.

Conversely, if `Kₙ = IⁿM^∧` for all `n ≥ 1`, then a Cauchy sequence `f` in `M^∧` converges: the
components `(f m) m` form a compatible family, because `f n - f m ∈ IᵐM^∧ ⊆ Kₘ` for `m ≤ n`, hence
define `L ∈ M^∧` with the same `k`-th component as `f k`; so `f k - L ∈ Kₖ = IᵏM^∧`. Together with
the fact that `M^∧` is always Hausdorff this gives completeness.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AdicCompletion.CompletionCriterion.solution` : Stacks 0318, as stated in
  `AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_iff_pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval`.

## Implementation notes

The source passes to inverse limits: the short exact sequences
`0 → Kₙ/IⁿM^∧ → M^∧/IⁿM^∧ → M/IⁿM → 0` form a tower with surjective transition maps on the
subobjects, so Stacks 03CA yields a short exact sequence
`0 → limₙ Kₙ/IⁿM^∧ → (M^∧)^∧ → M^∧ → 0`, and completeness of `M^∧` is the vanishing of that
limit, which for a tower of surjections is the vanishing of each term.

The argument carried out here is that argument with the limit taken by hand. The surjectivity of
`Kₙ₊₁ + IⁿM^∧ → Kₙ` is used in the form of the successive approximation above, the convergence of
the resulting series is precisely precompleteness of `M^∧`, and `x = L` replaces the injectivity of
`limₙ Kₙ/IⁿM^∧ → (M^∧)^∧`. Doing so avoids transporting the three towers, their maps and the
pointwise short exactness into `ℕᵒᵖ ⥤ ModuleCat R`, where the elements the argument manipulates are
no longer directly available; nothing is proved here that is not in the source's proof.

The successive approximation is separated from the completion it is applied to: it says that a
decreasing family of submodules of a precomplete module which has zero intersection, contains the
`I`-adic filtration and is generated stepwise by it is the `I`-adic filtration. Stated that way it
needs no lower bound on `n` and no hypothesis on `I`, and it is the only place completeness of
`M^∧` enters; the four properties of `{Kₙ}` it consumes are unconditional facts about a completion.
The converse direction genuinely uses the hypothesis for all `n ≥ 1`; at `n = 0` it is vacuous,
both sides being `⊤`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0318](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0318)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03CA](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03CA)
-/

namespace Stacks.AdicCompletion.CompletionCriterion

open Submodule _root_.AdicCompletion

section Filtration

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] (I : Ideal R) (N : Type*) [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N]

/-- A sequence whose consecutive differences lie in the successive steps `Iⁿ⁺ᵏN` of the `I`-adic
filtration telescopes: for `i ≤ j`, the difference `f j - f i` lies in `Iⁿ⁺ⁱN`. In particular such
a sequence is `I`-adically Cauchy. -/
private lemma sub_mem_pow_smul_top_of_sub_succ_mem (f : ℕ → N) {n : ℕ}
    (hf : ∀ k, f (k + 1) - f k ∈ I ^ (n + k) • (⊤ : Submodule R N)) {i j : ℕ} (hij : i ≤ j) :
    f j - f i ∈ I ^ (n + i) • (⊤ : Submodule R N) := by
  induction j, hij using Nat.le_induction with
  | base => simp
  | succ j hij ih =>
      rw [show f (j + 1) - f i = (f (j + 1) - f j) + (f j - f i) by abel]
      exact add_mem (Submodule.pow_smul_top_le I N (by omega) (hf j)) ih

/-- **Successive approximation.** Let `N` be an `I`-adically precomplete module and let
`K : ℕ → Submodule R N` be a decreasing family of submodules with zero intersection, each
containing the corresponding step `IᵏN` of the `I`-adic filtration and generated by `IᵏN` together
with its successor. Then `K` *is* the `I`-adic filtration.

Given `x ∈ Kₙ`, correcting it successively by elements of `Iⁿ⁺ᵏN` produces remainders `cₖ ∈ Kₙ₊ₖ`
whose partial sums `x - cₖ` lie in `IⁿN` and form a Cauchy sequence; its limit `L` lies in `IⁿN`
too, and `x - L` lies in every `Kₖ`, so `x = L`. -/
private lemma eq_pow_smul_top_of_le_sup_pow_smul_top [IsPrecomplete I N]
    (K : ℕ → Submodule R N) (hanti : Antitone K) (hbot : ⨅ k, K k = ⊥)
    (hle : ∀ k, I ^ k • (⊤ : Submodule R N) ≤ K k)
    (hsup : ∀ k, K k ≤ K (k + 1) ⊔ I ^ k • ⊤) (n : ℕ) :
    K n = I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule R N) := by
  classical
  refine le_antisymm (fun x hx => ?_) (hle n)
  have hsplit : ∀ k, ∀ y ∈ K k, ∃ z ∈ I ^ k • (⊤ : Submodule R N), y - z ∈ K (k + 1) := by
    intro k y hy
    obtain ⟨a, ha, z, hz, rfl⟩ := Submodule.mem_sup.1 (hsup k hy)
    exact ⟨z, hz, by simpa using ha⟩
  choose! g hg hgK using hsplit
  obtain ⟨c, hc₀, hc⟩ : ∃ c : ℕ → N, c 0 = x ∧ ∀ k, c (k + 1) = c k - g (n + k) (c k) :=
    ⟨fun k => Nat.rec x (fun k ck => ck - g (n + k) ck) k, rfl, fun _ => rfl⟩
  have hcK : ∀ k, c k ∈ K (n + k) := by
    intro k
    induction k with
    | zero => simpa [hc₀] using hx
    | succ k ih => rw [hc k]; exact hgK _ _ ih
  have htel : ∀ {i j : ℕ}, i ≤ j → (x - c j) - (x - c i) ∈ I ^ (n + i) • (⊤ : Submodule R N) :=
    fun {i j} hij => sub_mem_pow_smul_top_of_sub_succ_mem I N (fun k => x - c k)
      (fun k => by
        rw [show x - c (k + 1) - (x - c k) = g (n + k) (c k) by rw [hc k]; abel]
        exact hg _ _ (hcK k)) hij
  have hpart : ∀ k, x - c k ∈ I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule R N) := fun k => by
    simpa [hc₀] using htel (Nat.zero_le k)
  obtain ⟨L, hL⟩ := IsPrecomplete.prec ‹IsPrecomplete I N› (f := fun k => x - c k)
    fun {i j} hij => (SModEq.sub_mem.2
      (Submodule.pow_smul_top_le I N (Nat.le_add_left i n) (htel hij))).symm
  have hmem : ∀ k, x - L ∈ K k := fun k => by
    rw [show x - L = c k + ((x - c k) - L) by abel]
    exact add_mem (hanti (Nat.le_add_left k n) (hcK k)) (hle k (SModEq.sub_mem.1 (hL k)))
  rw [show x = L from sub_eq_zero.1 ((Submodule.mem_bot R).1
    (by rw [← hbot]; exact (Submodule.mem_iInf _).2 hmem))]
  simpa using sub_mem (hpart n) (SModEq.sub_mem.1 (hL n))

end Filtration

section Completion

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] (I : Ideal R) (M : Type*) [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]

/-- The kernels `Kₙ = Ker(M^∧ → M/IⁿM)` of the projections out of the `I`-adic completion of `M`
decrease with `n`. -/
private lemma antitone_ker_eval : Antitone fun n => (eval I M n).ker := by
  intro m n hmn x hx
  simp only [LinearMap.mem_ker, eval_apply] at hx ⊢
  rw [← x.prop hmn, hx, _root_.map_zero]

/-- An element of the `I`-adic completion of `M` all of whose components vanish is zero: the
kernels `Kₙ = Ker(M^∧ → M/IⁿM)` have zero intersection. -/
private lemma iInf_ker_eval_eq_bot :
    ⨅ n, (eval I M n).ker = (⊥ : Submodule R (AdicCompletion I M)) :=
  le_bot_iff.1 fun x hx => (Submodule.mem_bot R).2 <| AdicCompletion.ext fun k => by
    simpa [eval_apply] using (Submodule.mem_iInf _).1 hx k

/-- Two elements of the `I`-adic completion of `M` differ by an element of `Kₙ` if and only if
their `n`-th components agree. -/
private lemma sub_mem_ker_eval_iff {n : ℕ} {x y : AdicCompletion I M} :
    x - y ∈ (eval I M n).ker ↔ x.val n = y.val n := by
  simp [eval_apply, sub_eq_zero]

/-- The submodule `IⁿM^∧` of the `I`-adic completion of `M` maps onto `IⁿM/Iⁿ⁺¹M`, because
`M^∧ → M/Iⁿ⁺¹M` is surjective. So an element of `Kₙ = Ker(M^∧ → M/IⁿM)`, whose `(n+1)`-st
component lies in `IⁿM/Iⁿ⁺¹M`, can be corrected by an element of `IⁿM^∧` to lie in `Kₙ₊₁`; in
other words `Kₙ = Kₙ₊₁ + IⁿM^∧`. -/
private lemma ker_eval_le_ker_eval_succ_sup_pow_smul_top (n : ℕ) :
    (eval I M n).ker ≤ (eval I M (n + 1)).ker ⊔ I ^ n • ⊤ := by
  intro x hx
  obtain ⟨z, hz, hzx⟩ : x.val (n + 1) ∈ Submodule.map (eval I M (n + 1)) (I ^ n • ⊤) := by
    rw [Submodule.map_smul'', Submodule.map_top, range_eval]
    exact (val_apply_mem_smul_top_iff I n.le_succ).2 hx
  refine Submodule.mem_sup.2 ⟨x - z, ?_, z, hz, by abel⟩
  rw [sub_mem_ker_eval_iff, ← hzx, eval_apply]

/-- An `I`-adic Cauchy sequence `f` in the `I`-adic completion of `M` has a diagonal: its
components `(f m) m` form a compatible family, because `f n - f m` lies in `IᵐM^∧ ⊆ Kₘ` for
`m ≤ n`, so they are the components of an element of `M^∧`. -/
private lemma exists_val_apply_eq_of_smodEq (f : ℕ → AdicCompletion I M)
    (hf : ∀ {m n : ℕ}, m ≤ n → f m ≡ f n [SMOD I ^ m • (⊤ : Submodule R (AdicCompletion I M))]) :
    ∃ L : AdicCompletion I M, ∀ m, L.val m = (f m).val m := by
  have hval : ∀ {m n : ℕ}, m ≤ n → (f n).val m = (f m).val m := fun {m n} hmn =>
    (sub_mem_ker_eval_iff I M).1 <| pow_smul_top_le_ker_eval I m <| by
      simpa using neg_mem (SModEq.sub_mem.1 (hf hmn))
  exact ⟨⟨fun m => (f m).val m, fun {m n} hmn => by
    change transitionMap I M hmn ((f n).val n) = (f m).val m
    rw [(f n).prop hmn]
    exact hval hmn⟩, fun _ => rfl⟩

/-- If `Kₙ = Ker(M^∧ → M/IⁿM)` equals `IⁿM^∧` for every `n ≥ 1`, then the `I`-adic completion
`M^∧` of `M` is precomplete: the diagonal of a Cauchy sequence `f` agrees with `f k` in degree `k`,
so it is a limit of `f`. -/
private lemma isPrecomplete_of_pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval
    (h : ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n →
      I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule R (AdicCompletion I M)) = (eval I M n).ker) :
    IsPrecomplete I (AdicCompletion I M) := by
  refine ⟨fun f hf => ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨L, hL⟩ := exists_val_apply_eq_of_smodEq I M f hf
  refine ⟨L, fun k => ?_⟩
  rcases Nat.eq_zero_or_pos k with rfl | hk
  · rw [show (I ^ 0 • ⊤ : Submodule R (AdicCompletion I M)) = ⊤ by simp]
    exact SModEq.top
  · rw [SModEq.sub_mem, h k hk, sub_mem_ker_eval_iff, hL k]

end Completion

/-- The completion `AdicCompletion I M` is `I`-adically complete if and only if
`Iⁿ • AdicCompletion I M` is the kernel of `AdicCompletion.eval I M n` for every `n ≥ 1`; this is
the proof of `AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_iff_pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval`. -/
public theorem solution {R : Type*} [CommRing R] (I : Ideal R) (M : Type*) [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module R M] :
    IsAdicComplete I (AdicCompletion I M) ↔
      ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n →
        I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule R (AdicCompletion I M)) = (eval I M n).ker := by
  refine ⟨fun _ n _ => (eq_pow_smul_top_of_le_sup_pow_smul_top I (AdicCompletion I M)
      (fun k => (eval I M k).ker) (antitone_ker_eval I M) (iInf_ker_eval_eq_bot I M)
      (pow_smul_top_le_ker_eval I) (ker_eval_le_ker_eval_succ_sup_pow_smul_top I M) n).symm,
    fun h => ?_⟩
  have := isPrecomplete_of_pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval I M h
  exact {}

end Stacks.AdicCompletion.CompletionCriterion

/-!
# A criterion for the completion of a module to be adically complete

Let `R` be a ring, let `I ⊆ R` be an ideal and let `M` be an `R`-module, and write
`M^∧ = lim M/IⁿM` for the `I`-adic completion of `M`. The module `M^∧` need not be `I`-adically
complete: Stacks 05JA gives an example, and Stacks 05GG shows that finite generation of `I` is
enough to rule this out. What obstructs completeness is measured, filtration step by filtration
step, by the submodules

`Kₙ = Ker(M^∧ → M/IⁿM)`  and  `IⁿM^∧`

of `M^∧`. The second is always contained in the first, and `M^∧` is `I`-adically complete precisely
when the two agree for every `n ≥ 1`.

The mechanism is that `IⁿM^∧` maps onto `IⁿM/Iⁿ⁺¹M`, so that `Kₙ₊₁ + IⁿM^∧ = Kₙ` and the inverse
system `{Kₙ/IⁿM^∧}` has surjective transition maps; the exact sequences
`0 → Kₙ/IⁿM^∧ → M^∧/IⁿM^∧ → M/IⁿM → 0` then pass to the limit (Stacks 03CA) as a short exact
sequence `0 → limₙ Kₙ/IⁿM^∧ → (M^∧)^∧ → M^∧ → 0`. So the failure of `M^∧ → (M^∧)^∧` to be an
isomorphism is exactly the limit of the discrepancies `Kₙ/IⁿM^∧`, and, the transition maps being
surjective, that limit vanishes if and only if every one of the discrepancies does.

## Main results

* `AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete_iff_pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval`: for every ring `R`, every ideal
  `I` of `R` and every `R`-module `M`, the completion `AdicCompletion I M` is `I`-adically
  complete if and only if `Iⁿ • AdicCompletion I M` is the kernel of the projection
  `AdicCompletion.eval I M n` to `M ⧸ IⁿM` for all `n ≥ 1`.

## Implementation notes

The submodule `IⁿM` is written Mathlib's way, as `I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule R M)`, so that `IⁿM^∧` is
`I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule R (AdicCompletion I M))` and the source's `Kₙ`, the kernel of the
projection `M^∧ → M/IⁿM`, is the kernel of `AdicCompletion.eval I M n`. The equality is oriented
`IⁿM^∧ = Kₙ` to match its finitely generated case, which is Mathlib's
`AdicCompletion.pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval`; that theorem and `AdicCompletion.isAdicComplete` are
together the implication this criterion makes into an equivalence, so the content here is the
converse direction and the unconditional generality.

The source's `n ≥ 1` is kept, because that is the range in which the source's own proof reads off
vanishing of the discrepancies. It is not a restriction: `I⁰M = M`, so at `n = 0` both sides are
`⊤` and the equality holds for every `I` and `M`; hence the criterion is equally true with the
hypothesis `1 ≤ n` deleted.

The scalars are `R` throughout, as in the source. Along `R → R^∧` the `R`-submodule `IⁿM^∧` of
`M^∧` coincides with the `R^∧`-submodule `(IR^∧)ⁿM^∧`: an element of the latter is a sum
`∑ aᵢ • xᵢ` with `aᵢ ∈ (Iⁿ)R^∧`, and writing `aᵢ = ∑ⱼ cᵢⱼ • fⱼ` with `fⱼ ∈ Iⁿ` and `cᵢⱼ ∈ R^∧`
exhibits it as `∑ᵢⱼ fⱼ • (cᵢⱼ • xᵢ) ∈ IⁿM^∧`. For the same reason the two filtrations of `M^∧`
agree, so `IsAdicComplete I (AdicCompletion I M)` is also `I R^∧`-adic completeness of `M^∧`.

Faithfulness. (1) No hypothesis is dead: the lemma has none beyond the data `R`, `I`, `M`, all of
which occur in the conclusion, and that is the point of it — unlike Stacks 05GG and 031C it assumes
neither that `I` is finitely generated nor that `R` is Noetherian. (2) The scalars and base are the
source's: `M` is an `R`-module, `Iⁿ • ⊤` and the kernel are `R`-submodules of `M^∧`, and the
completeness predicate is taken for the ideal `I` of `R` (see the paragraph above for the
comparison with `R^∧`). (3) The canonical maps the source names are pinned rather than asserted to
exist: `Kₙ` is the kernel of the projection `AdicCompletion.eval I M n`, whose value at `x` is the
`n`-th component `x.1 n` of `x` as an element of the limit, and `IsAdicComplete I (AdicCompletion I
M)` is bijectivity of the canonical map `AdicCompletion.of I (AdicCompletion I M)` from `M^∧` to
`(M^∧)^∧` (`AdicCompletion.of_bijective_iff`), which is the source's `(M^∧)^∧ ≃ M^∧`. (4) The
instantiation is not degenerate, and neither side of the equivalence is: for `R = ℤ`, `I = (p)` and
`M = ℤ` the completion is the ring `ℤ_p` of `p`-adic integers, which is `p`-adically complete, and
indeed `pⁿℤ_p = Ker(ℤ_p → ℤ/pⁿ)`; whereas for `R = k[x₁, x₂, x₃, …]`, `M = R` and
`I = (x₁, x₂, x₃, …)` the completion `R^∧` is not `I`-adically complete (Stacks 05JA) and
correspondingly `IR^∧` is strictly smaller than `Ker(R^∧ → R/I)`.

## References

* [StackProject 0318](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0318), which attributes the lemma to an
  unpublished note of Lenstra and de Smit
* [StackProject 05GG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05GG) for the finitely generated case
* [StackProject 05JA](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05JA) for an example where the criterion
  fails

## Tags

adic completion, complete, Lenstra, de Smit
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace AdicCompletion

/-- **Stacks 0318.** Let `R` be a ring, let `I ⊆ R` be an ideal and let `M` be an `R`-module.
Write `Kₙ = Ker(M^∧ → M/IⁿM)` for the kernel of the `n`-th projection out of the `I`-adic
completion `M^∧` of `M`. Then `M^∧` is `I`-adically complete if and only if `Kₙ = IⁿM^∧` for all
`n ≥ 1`. -/
@[stacks 0318 "The criterion of Lenstra and de Smit; no hypothesis on `I` or `R`."]
public theorem isAdicComplete_iff_pow_smul_top_eq_ker_eval
    {R : Type*} [CommRing R] (I : Ideal R) (M : Type*) [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] :
    IsAdicComplete I (AdicCompletion I M) ↔
      ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n →
        I ^ n • (⊤ : Submodule R (AdicCompletion I M)) = (eval I M n).ker :=
  Stacks.AdicCompletion.CompletionCriterion.solution I M

end AdicCompletion

end
